activation	Print prompt	2026-10-27T11:58:57.9529047Z {"title": "`INTERNAL Error: Attempted to access index 0 within vector of size 0` from `StatisticsPropagator::TryExecuteAggregates` on scalar `MAX(...) WHERE` subquery against same table", "body": "### What happens?\n\nIn DuckDB **v1.5.2**, a query of the form\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM t\n WHERE col = (SELECT MAX(col) FROM t WHERE col <= <literal>);\n```\n\naborts at plan time with\n\n```\nINTERNAL Error: Attempted to access index 0 within vector of size 0\n```\n\nwhen:\n\n1. `t` contains at least one row, **and**\n2. the inner subquery's `WHERE` clause prunes every row from `t`'s row-group statistics (i.e. the optimizer can prove the aggregate is over zero partitions).\n\nThe crash happens inside `StatisticsPropagator::TryExecuteAggregates` while trying to statically evaluate the inner aggregate \u2014 `partition_stats[0]` is dereferenced on an empty `vector<PartitionStatistics>`. This signals an `InternalException`, not a planner error to be caught and recovered from.\n\n### Minimal repro\n\n```sql\nINSTALL duckdb; -- v1.5.2\nCREATE TABLE t (d DATE);\nINSERT INTO t VALUES (DATE '2026-05-16');\n\nSELECT * FROM t\n WHERE d = (SELECT MAX(d) FROM t WHERE d <= DATE '2025-03-15');\n-- INTERNAL Error: Attempted to access index 0 within vector of size 0\n```\n\nRemoving **any one** of these makes it succeed:\n\n| Change                                                                 | Result                  |\n|------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------|\n| `DELETE FROM t` (empty table)                                            | OK \u2014 returns 0 rows     |\n| `WHERE d <= DATE '2099-01-01'` (predicate matches)                       | OK                      |\n| `MAX(...)` from a different table than the outer                         | also fails (same error) |\n| `(SELECT d FROM t WHERE d <= DATE '2025-03-15' ORDER BY d DESC LIMIT 1)` | OK                      |\n| `(SELECT MAX(d) FROM t HAVING MAX(d) <= DATE '2025-03-15')`              | OK                      |\n\nSo the trigger is specifically: a scalar aggregate subquery whose `WHERE` predicate prunes the entire source table's row-group statistics. It fires regardless of how the result is consumed (predicate, `SELECT` list, etc.) and regardless of wrapping (`OFFSET 0`, double subselect, CTE, explicit `CAST`).\n\n### Stack trace (abridged)\n\n```\nduckdb::vector<duckdb::PartitionStatistics, \u2026>::operator[](unsigned long) + 200\nduckdb::StatisticsPropagator::TryExecuteAggregates(duckdb::LogicalAggregate&, \u2026) + 2788\nduckdb::StatisticsPropagator::PropagateStatistics(duckdb::LogicalAggregate&, \u2026)\n\u2026\nduckdb::Optimizer::RunBuiltInOptimizers() + 2140\nduckdb::Optimizer::Optimize(\u2026) + 244\nduckdb::ClientContext::CreatePreparedStatementInternal(\u2026)\nduckdb::ClientContext::PendingQuery(\u2026)\n```\n\n### Workaround\n\n`SET disabled_optimizers = 'statistics_propagation';` on the connection avoids the crash but is a fairly heavy hammer. The targeted workaround is to rewrite the scalar aggregate as `ORDER BY ... LIMIT 1`:\n\n```sql\n-- before\n(SELECT MAX(d) FROM t WHERE d <= ?)\n-- after\n(SELECT d FROM t WHERE d <= ? ORDER BY d DESC LIMIT 1)\n```\n\nwhich is semantically equivalent (both return `NULL` when no rows match) and isn't subject to the static-aggregate optimization path that aborts.\n\n\n### To Reproduce\n\n```\nCREATE TABLE t (d DATE);\nINSERT INTO t VALUES (DATE '2026-05-16');\n\nSELECT * FROM t\n WHERE d = (SELECT MAX(d) FROM t WHERE d <= DATE '2025-03-15');\n-- INTERNAL Error: Attempted to access index 0 within vector of size 0\n```\n\n### OS:\n\naarch64\n\n### DuckDB Version:\n\n1.5.3\n\n### DuckDB Client:\n\nCLI\n\n### Hardware:\n\n_No response_\n\n### Full Name:\n\nRyan Schmukler\n\n### Affiliation:\n\nDynamic Alpha Technologies Inc.\n\n### Did you include all relevant configuration (e.g., CPU architecture, Linux distribution) to reproduce the issue?\n\n- [ ] Yes, I have\n\n### Did you include all code required to reproduce the issue?\n\n- [ ] Yes, I have\n\n### Did you include all relevant data sets for reproducing the issue?\n\nNo - Other reason (please specify in the issue body)"}
